# GitHub & Git strategy (agent must follow)

**SSOT docs (read when unsure):** `docs/BRANCHING_STRATEGY.md`, `docs/VERSIONING_POLICY.md`, `docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md`

The product owner is not a programmer. After major or fundamental work (or a coherent series of related changes), **you must commit and push** using this strategy—do not leave durable progress uncommitted unless the user says to wait.

## When to commit and push (timing)

Do this automatically when any of these are true:

1. A feature/fix/chore slice is **functionally complete** (builds, hooks pass, intent delivered).
2. A **series of related edits** forms one logical unit (e.g. DI triage + migrations + i18n).
3. Work is **risky to lose** (schema, IPC, auth, migrations, multi-file refactors).
4. The user asks to save/push progress, or ends a major task without an open “hold commits” request.

Do **not** spam micro-commits for every tiny edit mid-debug; batch into one conventional commit per logical unit. Prefer **one clear commit** over many noisy ones for the same slice.

### Session commit discipline (mandatory)

**No feature may remain uncommitted for more than one logical work session.**

If a feature becomes stable (builds, intent clear, safe to preserve):

1. Ensure a dedicated feature branch exists (one feature).
2. Commit.
3. Push.

Do **not** accumulate unrelated work across modules in one working tree. Do **not** work for days/weeks with only a dirty tree or stash — that is how irreversible mess starts. Official mirror: `docs/BRANCHING_STRATEGY.md`.

## Branch model (never violate)

```
feature|fix|chore → develop (PR) → staging → main (PR)
hotfix from release tag on main → PR to main + PR to develop
```

- Work only on `feature/*`, `fix/*`, `chore/*`, or `hotfix/*` — never commit directly to `main`, `staging`, or `develop`.
- If detached/wrong branch: create/switch to the correct branch **before** committing; never rewrite protected history.
- Keep existing feature branch when already on one (e.g. `feature/p5-sp00-…`); do not invent a parallel branch for the same sprint unless asked.

### One branch = one feature

- Each `feature/*` branch owns **one** coherent feature or sprint slice (e.g. AssetPicker only).
- Do not pile unrelated recoveries, refactors, or modules onto an existing feature branch.
- Prefer small, purpose-named branches over long-lived multi-feature stacks.

### No duplicate feature branches

Before creating any feature/fix/chore branch:

1. Search local and remote branches (`git branch -a`, name/slug/`p{N}-sp{NN}` match).
2. If a branch already exists for the same feature or sprint → **continue there**.
3. Never create a near-duplicate (`feature/p2-sp04-di-…` vs `feature/p2-sp04-di-lifecycle-…` for the same work).
4. If uncertain whether an existing branch covers the work → **ask before branching**.

### Temporary integration / cleanup branches

Names like `cleanup/*`, `reconstruction/*`, or similar are **temporary integration branches**, not long-lived development lines.

- Allowed briefly during history rebuild or multi-commit reconstruction.
- Target merge: **`cleanup|reconstruction` → `develop` (PR)** as soon as the slice is complete.
- After a **successful merge into `develop`**: archive if an audit trail is needed, otherwise **delete** the branch (local + remote after merge).
- They must **never** become standing bases for ongoing feature work.

### Feature branches must not depend on cleanup long-term

During a rebuild it is acceptable that features temporarily stack:

```
develop → cleanup/… → feature/di-…
```

After cleanup merges into `develop`, the steady state must be:

```
develop
├── feature/x
├── feature/y
└── feature/z
```

Not `feature → cleanup → feature → feature`.

- New work after cleanup lands: branch from **updated `develop`**, not from the deleted cleanup tip.
- Rebase/retarget open feature PRs onto `develop` if they were created atop cleanup.
- Do not open new features off a cleanup branch once cleanup is merged or obsolete.

### Recommended merge order (reconstruction / isolated recoveries)

When a temporary cleanup and isolated one-feature recoveries coexist:

```
cleanup/… → develop
feature/di-… → develop
(later) feature/…-asset-picker → develop
(later) feature/…-procurement-archive-… → develop
```

Merge one logical unit at a time. Keep unrelated recovered features **isolated** until deliberately integrated.

## Stashes are temporary only (mandatory)

**A stash must never become the only copy (SSOT) of product code.**

Before dropping, popping, or replacing any stash, the agent **MUST** verify that every unique change is already reachable from at least one Git reference (branch, tag, or permanent commit on a named ref).

If any unique code exists only inside the stash:

1. Create a dedicated feature branch (one feature).
2. Restore the content.
3. Commit it.
4. Then drop the stash.

Never rely on dangling Git objects as backup.

### Safe stash removal

Never execute `git stash drop` or `git stash clear` until the agent explicitly reports:

```text
SAFE TO DELETE
```

Including:

- reachability verified
- no unique product code remains only in the stash
- recovery no longer required

## Reachability check (mandatory)

Before deleting any branch, stash, or temporary ref (`safety/*`, `compare/*`, `recovery/*`, `cleanup/*` after merge), perform a reachability audit.

Verify:

- every commit worth keeping is reachable from a permanent branch (or intentional archive tag/ref)
- no product code exists only in dangling commits
- no unique blobs exist only in unreachable objects

If any unique code exists, preserve it first (dedicated feature branch + commit), then delete.

## Pre-reconstruction / pre-recovery snapshot (mandatory)

Before **any** reconstruction or recovery operation, run and **save** a baseline report:

```bash
git status
git branch --all
git stash list
git log --graph --decorate --oneline --all -50
git tag
```

Persist the full output to a dated file under `docs/engineering/git-snapshots/` (e.g. `YYYYMMDD-HHMM-pre-recovery.md`) and/or the session end-of-task report so a rollback baseline always exists. Do not start destructive recovery until the report is saved.

## Recovery branches

If a recovered feature is **unrelated** to the current work:

- Do **not** merge it into the current branch.
- Create a dedicated feature branch.
- Restore the **exact** original content (byte-identical when forensic recovery requires it).
- Commit without opportunistic refactoring.
- Continue current work independently.

Recovered features must become first-class Git history (named branch + commit), not stash or dangling leftovers.

## Branch health check

After every sprint, and **before starting any new sprint**, generate a **Branch Health Report**:

| Section | Include |
|---------|---------|
| Active feature branches | Purpose + tip SHA |
| Temporary integration branches | e.g. `cleanup/*` — merge or delete? |
| Archived branches | Intentional keep |
| Scratch branches | `safety/*`, `compare/*`, `recovery/*` — local only |
| Stale branches | remote `[gone]`, abandoned sprints |
| Merge order | Recommended next merges |
| Dependency graph | What is stacked on what (must not stay stacked on cleanup) |

Recommend branches that should disappear. Prevent multi-year clutter.

## Repository inventory

At the end of every major feature or sprint, generate a **repository inventory**:

- feature / merged / active / archived / dropped branches
- dangling commits and recovery risks
- highlight anything that may disappear after `git gc`

Keep the repository organized as Maintafox grows.

## Commit message style (unified conventional)

Format:

```text
type(scope): short imperative summary

- optional body bullets: why / what modules
```

| type | use for |
|------|---------|
| `feat` | new capability |
| `fix` | bug fix |
| `chore` | gitignore, deps, tooling, non-app docs |
| `docs` | documentation only |
| `refactor` | no behavior change |
| `test` | tests only |
| `perf` | performance |

- Scope = module area: `di`, `org`, `auth`, `admin`, `wo`, `gitignore`, `phase5`, etc.
- Subject: imperative, ≤ ~72 chars, no trailing period.
- Match recent repo style (e.g. `feat(di,lookups,auth): …`, `chore(gitignore): …`).

## Safe commit procedure (mandatory checklist)

1. `git status` + `git diff` + `git log -5` (style check).
2. Stage **only source of truth**: app code, migrations, shared IPC, docs intended for the change.
3. **Never stage:** secrets (`.env`, credentials), `node_modules/`, `src-tauri/target/`, `src-tauri/target-*`, `target-check*`, build caches, IDE junk, large generated binaries unless the user explicitly wants them.
4. If huge untracked trees appear under `target*` / `target-check*`: **do not** `git add -A`; stage paths explicitly; recommend/add `.gitignore` entries in a separate `chore(gitignore): …` commit.
5. Commit with HEREDOC/`-m` multi-line conventional message (PowerShell-safe on Windows).
6. If pre-commit/lint-staged fails: **fix the issue**, create a **new** commit — never `--no-verify`, never amend a failed commit — **except** the exact-recovery case below.
7. `git push -u origin HEAD` (or push current feature branch). **No force-push** to `main`/`staging`/`develop`. No force-push elsewhere unless the user explicitly orders it.
8. Confirm clean tree for intended files; report commit SHA + branch + remote result.

### Exact-recovery `--no-verify` exception

If pre-commit fails because the agent **intentionally restores historical code byte-identically** (repository recovery, forensic reconstruction, history preservation), the agent **MAY** use `--no-verify` **only if all** of the following are true:

1. The user requested exact recovery (or the recovery plan requires byte-identical blobs).
2. The restored blob must remain byte-identical (lint/format would destroy forensic equality).
3. The reason is documented in the commit message and/or the final task report.

Otherwise, skipping hooks remains **forbidden**. Do not weaken hooks for ordinary feature work.

## Branch naming and work organization

Allowed names only: `feature/p{N}-sp{NN}-{slug}`, `fix/{issue-id}-{slug}`, `chore/{slug}`, `hotfix/{version}-{slug}`.

Temporary integration names (`cleanup/*`, reconstruction-style) are allowed only under the temporary-integration rules above and must not linger after merge to `develop`.

- One branch per sprint/logical unit; stay on the existing sprint branch instead of forking a near-duplicate.
- Before committing, verify the branch name matches the current work; if it does not, create a correctly named branch from the current tip and continue there.
- Backup/scratch refs (`safety/*`, `compare/*`, `recovery/*`, `backup-*`) are **local only** — never push them and never name them `v*`.
- Keep merged sprint branches for 30 days (audit trail). Delete only stale local scratch refs, and only after the **reachability check**.
- After cleanup/reconstruction merges to `develop`, delete (or archive then delete) that integration branch promptly.
- Report stale/diverged branches (remote `[gone]`, unmerged old sprint branches) instead of silently deleting or force-updating them.

## Porting fixes already made on other branches

When a fix exists on another branch, **reuse it — never re-implement or overwrite it**:

1. Locate it: `git log --all --oneline --grep="<keyword>"` or `git branch -a --contains <sha>`.
2. Bring it over with `git cherry-pick -x <sha>` (keeps provenance) onto the correct target branch.
3. If the fix only lives in a stash/scratch ref, preserve it on a dedicated feature branch (commit), then verify nothing else from that ref is lost — stash is not SSOT.
4. Never merge an old branch wholesale to grab one fix, and never revert newer work to make an old branch apply cleanly.
5. Resolve conflicts by keeping current architecture; state clearly in the report which commits were ported.

## Pull requests and GitHub hygiene

- One PR per branch toward `develop` (draft is fine). Update the existing PR — never open duplicates; refresh its title/body when scope grows.
- PR title format from `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`: `[P{N}-SP{NN}-F{NN}-S{N}] Description`; fill Summary, Changes Made, and the Definition of Done checklist.
- Apply existing labels from `.github/labels.yml` only (`phase:*`, `type:*`, `status:*`, `priority:*`) — do not invent labels.
- Use `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/` for bugs and supervisor feedback; link issues in `fix/*` PRs.
- Respect required status checks in `.github/branch-protection.md` (`develop` 1 approval, `staging` 1, `main` 2). Never bypass or merge red CI.
- Report CI failures with the cause; fix forward in new commits.

## Version and release management

**Every release must be reproducible.**

- Only **merge-tested** commits (through the promotion path) may receive a version tag.
- Never tag temporary `cleanup/*` or reconstruction branches.
- Never tag unmerged feature tips, stash tips, or scratch refs.

Only during an explicit release/versioning task:

1. Follow the phase ranges in `docs/VERSIONING_POLICY.md`; bump `package.json`, `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`, and `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json` to the **identical** string in one commit.
2. Move `CHANGELOG.md` `[Unreleased]` into a dated version section; `[Unreleased]` must never be empty in a PR to `main`.
3. Complete `docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md` gates, then promote `develop` → `staging` → `main` by PR (2 approvals for `main`).
4. After the merge to `main`, create the annotated tag there and push it separately:
   `git tag -a v{VERSION} -m "Maintafox {VERSION} — {summary}"` then `git push origin --tags`.
5. Publish the GitHub release from that tag (`gh release create v{VERSION} …`), verify installer artifacts, and keep it draft until artifacts are confirmed.
6. Never tag or publish a release from `feature/*`, `fix/*`, `develop`, `staging`, or `cleanup/*`; if the user asks for a release before promotion, explain the gap and offer the promotion path.

## Hard forbidden actions

| Action | Status |
|--------|--------|
| Direct commit to `main` / `staging` / `develop` | Forbidden |
| Force-push protected branches | Never |
| Amend/rebase **pushed** shared commits | Forbidden |
| `--no-verify` / skip hooks | Forbidden except exact-recovery exception above |
| Using a stash as the only copy of product code | Forbidden |
| `git stash drop` / `git stash clear` without `SAFE TO DELETE` report | Forbidden |
| Leaving `cleanup/*` (or similar) as a long-lived base after merge to `develop` | Forbidden |
| Creating a duplicate branch for an existing feature/sprint | Forbidden — continue or ask |
| `reset --hard` / destructive discard of user work | Forbidden without explicit user order + backup |
| Commit secrets or build artifact trees | Forbidden |
| Official release tags off `main` | Forbidden |
| Version tags on `cleanup/*`, reconstruction, feature, or scratch tips | Forbidden |
| Leaving stable work uncommitted across sessions / multi-module dirty trees | Forbidden |
| Starting reconstruction/recovery without a saved baseline snapshot | Forbidden |
| Deleting branches/refs holding unpushed / unique work | Forbidden without reachability check (+ user order when destructive) |
| Re-implementing a fix that already exists on another branch | Forbidden — cherry-pick it |
| Merging an unrelated recovered feature into the current WIP branch | Forbidden — dedicated recovery branch |

## End-of-task report (always)

After auto commit/push, tell the user briefly:

- commit SHA + message
- branch pushed
- PR link if any
- what was excluded (e.g. local `target-check*`)
- commits ported from other branches, if any
- if a release tag was **not** created, say why (tags only on `main`)
- any branch/organization problem noticed (bad name, stale branch, diverged `develop`, cleanup still living, features still stacked on cleanup)
- if hooks were skipped: exact-recovery justification
- after major sprints: point to or include Branch Health / Repository Inventory

```text
❌ BAD — leave a finished multi-file feature uncommitted; or git add -A including target-check
❌ BAD — stash as SSOT; long-lived cleanup base; duplicate feature branches; drop stash without SAFE TO DELETE
❌ BAD — weeks of multi-module WIP with no commits; recovery without a saved baseline snapshot
✅ GOOD — conventional commit of the logical unit on feature/*, push, keep/update PR to develop
✅ GOOD — one branch = one feature; recoveries on dedicated branches; cleanup merged then deleted
✅ GOOD — session-end commit+push when stable; pre-recovery snapshot saved under docs/engineering/git-snapshots/
```
